445162873e-07,0.0,-2.9801878554280847e-05,-0.0005891970940865576],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,32,26931,10486,702,8356,46342,11,714,279,8622,9210,57960,15976,3,323,10578,400,81,3,525,5420,311,13289,382,58,6405,921,25643,551,7,17,9961,626,12670,362,11,425,11,506,401,32,284,320,16,11,15,317,33,284,5419,7,16,16,15,317,46,284,320,15,11,15,626,7633,4346,313,46,313,33,317,7633,91958,19238,11,16,11,15,11,16,16,15,3237,1502,20912,81,73315,320,46,488,362,5620,17,11,328,317,1502,20912,81,73315,320,46,488,425,5620,17,11,13387,317,1502,20912,59,15976,73315,320,15,13,16,11,15,13,16,20,1106,24157,6405,2533,23085,8622,9210,57960,15976,3,30027,4756,279,3082,315,279,26931,10486,30,220,20678,279,4226,304,50784,382,9112,25,576,15580,3084,315,279,10486,374,400,81,1124,15976,4779,323,279,3082,315,279,10486,374,57960,37018,90,81,61,17,1124,15976,15170,17,92,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA circular sector has fixed perimeter, but the central angle $\\theta$ and radius $r$ are allowed to vary.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(2 cm);\n\npair A, B, O;\n\nA = (1,0);\nB = dir(110);\nO = (0,0);\n\ndraw(A--O--B);\ndraw(arc(O,1,0,110));\n\nlabel(\"$r$\", (O + A)/2, S);\nlabel(\"$r$\", (O + B)/2, SW);\nlabel(\"$\\theta$\", (0.1,0.15));\n[/asy]\n\nWhich central angle $\\theta$ maximizes the area of the circular sector?  Give the answer in radians.\n\nNote: The arc length of the sector is $r \\theta,$ and the area of the sector is $\\frac{r^2 \\theta}{2}.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"abee7bd1b5ab52f4af73b3208ad3b7dca9a79a2fda15c66f48ef3c89722218cb"},"seconds":12.660578489303589,"seed":502862099786827346,"stop":"eos","text":"To maximize the area of the circular sector with a fixed perimeter, we start by expressing the perimeter and the area in terms of the radius \\( r \\) and the central angle \\( \\theta \\).\n\nThe perimeter of the sector is the sum of the two radii and the arc length:\n\\[\n2r + r\\theta = P\n\\]\nwhere \\( P \\) is the fixed perimeter. We can solve this equation for \\( \\theta \\):\n\\[\nr\\theta = P - 2r \\implies \\theta = \\frac{P - 2r}{r}\n\\]\n\nThe area \\( A \\) of the sector is given by:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{1}{2} r^2 \\theta\n\\]\nSubstituting the expression for \\( \\theta \\) from the perimeter equation into the area formula, we get:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{1}{2} r^2 \\left( \\frac{P - 2r}{r} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} r (P - 2r) = \\frac{1}{2} Pr - r^2\n\\]\n\nTo find the value of \\( r \\) that maximizes the area, we take the derivative of \\( A \\) with respect to \\( r \\) and set it to zero:\n\\[\n\\frac{dA}{dr} = \\frac{1}{2} P - 2r\n\\]\nSetting the derivative equal to zero gives:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} P - 2r = 0 \\implies 2r = \\frac{1}{2} P \\implies r = \\frac{P}{4}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( r = \\frac{P}{4} \\) back into the expression for \\( \\theta \\):\n\\[\n\\theta = \\frac{P - 2 \\left( \\frac{P}{4} \\right)}{\\frac{P}{4}} = \\frac{P - \\frac{P}{2}}{\\frac{P}{4}} = \\frac{\\frac{P}{2}}{\\frac{P}{4}} = \\frac{P}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{P} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the central angle \\( \\theta \\) that maximizes the area of the circular sector is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:605"}
